 ## Overview
-Truncated Depth-Based Sampling (TDBS) is a ray sampling strategy that utilizes monocular depth priors informed by the truncated normal distribution to optimize ray point sampling, improving convergence speed and accuracy of pose estimation [S1]. It differs from siblings like [NoPe-NeRF](link) by explicitly using the truncated normal distribution for sampling to fully exploit depth priors, whereas NoPe-NeRF considers overall depth distortion and does not fully exploit the potential of monocular depth maps [S1]. It builds on [Depth-Guided Sampling](7490-depth-guided-sampling.md) by replacing uniform or heuristic sampling with a probabilistic model centered on the estimated depth.
+Truncated Depth-Based Sampling (TDBS) is a ray sampling strategy that utilizes monocular depth priors informed by the truncated normal distribution to optimize ray point sampling, improving convergence speed and accuracy of pose estimation [S1]. It differs from siblings like [NoPe-NeRF](8866-pixel-region-and-depth-guided-ray-sampling.md) by explicitly using the truncated normal distribution for sampling to fully exploit depth priors, whereas NoPe-NeRF considers overall depth distortion and does not fully exploit the potential of monocular depth maps [S1]. It builds on [Depth-Guided Sampling](7490-depth-guided-sampling.md) by using a coarse monocular depth map as a prior to sample points from a truncated normal distribution, assuming the real surface is near the estimated depth [S1].
 
